1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,364
This is Symfony Code Mode.

2
00:00:02,364 --> 00:00:06,000
I ask Codex to audit published articles.

3
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:10,000
It writes PHP to combine approved MCP
tools.

4
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:15,268
Then I ask which shared-image fixes help
the most articles.

5
00:00:15,268 --> 00:00:20,000
Same conversation, new computation, same
permissions.

6
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:23,184
This saved rehearsal shows both turns.

7
00:00:23,184 --> 00:00:26,872
The follow-up rechecked forty-eight
articles

8
00:00:26,872 --> 00:00:32,067
and found three shared-image fixes, each
helping two articles.

9
00:00:32,067 --> 00:00:35,000
Here is the exact PHP it submitted.

10
00:00:35,000 --> 00:00:38,164
The host recorded ten upstream calls:

11
00:00:38,164 --> 00:00:43,296
four list pages, four article batches and
two media batches.

12
00:00:43,296 --> 00:00:48,000
Expand PHP execute for the actual request
and response.

13
00:00:48,000 --> 00:00:52,663
A separate Symfony AI reference comparison
returns the same findings

14
00:00:52,663 --> 00:00:57,394
with ten calls each and eighty-five
percent less tool-result payload.

15
00:00:57,394 --> 00:01:00,000
That measures bytes, not model tokens.

16
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:03,422
These fixed probes ran real PHP in gVisor.

17
00:01:03,422 --> 00:01:07,741
An approved read succeeded. A delete call
was denied.

18
00:01:07,741 --> 00:01:11,407
An infinite loop hit the two-second
watchdog.

19
00:01:11,407 --> 00:01:14,422
Each container was confirmed removed.

20
00:01:14,422 --> 00:01:18,089
The guest has no network or host
credentials.

21
00:01:18,089 --> 00:01:22,000
These checks are evidence, not a security
audit.

22
00:01:22,000 --> 00:01:26,035
A reusable Composer package for Symfony AI
and MCP agents.

23
00:01:26,035 --> 00:01:30,000
Conversations stay in Codex; the host
controls execution.
